          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p In short:
          %p
            At sufficiently low
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/temperature"} temperature
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electron"} electron
            systems (such as at
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semiconductor"} semiconductor
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "semiconductor#ReferencesHeterostructures"} heterojunctions
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum effects
            change the nature of the classical
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hall+effect"} Hall effect
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                in the
                %em integral
                quantum Hall effect, quantization of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/energy"} energy
                into
                %em Landau levels
                of electrons (that are circulating in a transverse
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magnetic+field"} magnetic field
                while confined to a plane) causes the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hall+effect"} Hall resistance
                — which classically increases linearly with increasing external
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magnetic+field"} magnetic field
                — to instead intermittently form constant “plateaus” as the Landau levels get “filled” by electron states,
            %li
              %p
                in the
                = succeed "," do
                  %em fractional quantum Hall effect
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bound+states"} bound states
                “with” magnetic
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+quantization"} flux quanta
                that may exhibit
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+field+theory"} effective
                %em fractional
                charge and, apparently, “fractional statistics” (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anyon"} anyonic
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braiding"} braiding
                behaviour).
          %p
            %br/
          %p In a tad more detail:
          %p
            %br/
          %p#FirstIdea
            %strong Quantum Hall effect.
            In a
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∼
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim
            2D sheet
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Σ
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma^2
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conductor+%28electromagnetism%29"} conducting
            material threaded by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magnetic+field"} magnetic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+density"} flux density
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
          %p
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/energy"} energy
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electron"} electron
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+states"} quantum states
            is quantized by
            %em Landau levels
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \in \mathbb{N}
            as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi ℏ
                  %msub
                    %mi ω
                    %mi B
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mo +
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mn 2
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  E
                  \\,=\,
                  \\hbar \omega_B
                  \\big(
                  i + \tfrac{1}{2}
                  \\big)
                  \\,,
          %p
            where each level comprises one state per magnetic
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+quantum"} flux quantum
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi n
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} deg
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %msub
                    %mi Φ
                    %mn 0
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi B
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                    %mfrac
                      %mi e
                      %mi h
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  n_{\mathrm{deg}}
                  \\,=\,
                  B/\Phi_0 \,=\, B \tfrac{e}{h}
                  \\,.
          %p
            %strong Integer quantum Hall effect.
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Landau%27s+Fermi+liquid+theory"} Fermi theory
            of idealized
            %em free
            electrons hence predicts the system to be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conductor+%28electromagnetism%29"} conductor
            away from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/energy+gaps"} energy gaps
            between a completely filled and the next empty Landau level, hence away from the number of electrons being integer multiples
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi n
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} el
                    %mo =
                    %mi ν
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %msub
                      %mi Φ
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n_{\mathrm{el}} = \nu B/\Phi_0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ν
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu \in \mathbb{N}
            of the number of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux"} flux
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+quantization"} quanta
          %p
            This is indeed observed and is called the
            %em integer quantum Hall effect
            — in fact the vanishing conductivity is observed in sizeable neighbourhoods of the critical filling fractions (“Hall plateaux”, attributed to disorder effects).
          %p
            %strong Fractional quantum Hall effect.
            But electrons in a conductor are far from free. While there is little to no theory for strongly interacting quantum systems,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiment"} experiment
            shows that the Fermi idealization breaks down at low enough temperature, where longitudinal conductivity decreases also in neighbourhoods of certain
            %em fractional
            filling factors
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ν
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ∈
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi ℚ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu \,\in\, \mathbb{Q}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ν
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu = 1/k
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo ∈
                    %mn 2
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \in 2\mathbb{N} + 1
          %p
            The heuristic idea is that at these filling fractions the interacting electrons form a kind of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bound+state"} bound state
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            flux quanta each, making “composite
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bosons"} bosons
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bose-Einstein+condensate"} condense
            to produce an insulating
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass+gap"} mass gap
          %p#IntroAnyonicQuasiParticles
            %strong Anyonic quasi-particles.
            But this suggests that in the Hall plateau neighbourhood around such filling fraction, there are unpaired
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux"} flux
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quanta"} quanta
            each “bound to” one
            = succeed "th" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1/k
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-particles"} quasi-particles
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/FluxQuantaAsAnyons.png", :width => "520px"}/
              %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"}
                (graphics from
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "#SS25AlgTop"} SS25
          %p
            These evidently have fractional charge
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ±
                  %mi e
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pm e/k
            and are expected to be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anyon"} anyonic
            with pair exchange phase
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi e
                      %mrow
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} i
                        %mi π
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e^{\mathrm{i} \pi/k}
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#BraidingPhase"} below
          %p
            %br/
          %h3#IdeaHallResistivity Hall effect and Hall resistivity
          %p
            The setup of any
            %em Hall effect
            is a plane sheet of (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semiconductor"}> semi-
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conductor+%28electromagnetism%29"} conducting
            material placed in a transverse
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magnetic+field"} magnetic field
            (constant across the plane, directed perpendicular to it).
          %p
            The
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+Hall+effect"} classical Hall effect
            is the phenomenon that a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/voltage"} voltage
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_x
            applied along the conducting sheet in some direction – to be called the
            = succeed "-direction" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            %em Hall voltage
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_y
            in the
            %em perpendicular
            direction – to be called the
            = succeed "-direction" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
          %p
            The cause of this effect is the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lorentz+force"} Lorentz force
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electrons"} electrons
            by the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magnetic+field"} magnetic field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/velocity"} velocity
            but perpendicular in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direction+vector"} direction
            to both the magnetic field and to their direction of motion.
          %p Due to this force, the electrons which start to follow the applied voltage gradient quickly drift to one side of the conducting sheet until their mutual electrostatic repulsion there counterbalances the Lorentz force. At this point the electrons move straight along the applied voltage gradient, with the Lorentz force now exactly compensated by the Hall voltage due to the gradient in electron concentration.
          %p
            For more details on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+Hall+effect"} classical Hall effect
            see there; here we further just need the formulas for conductivity and resistivity:
          %p
            Consider in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/plane"} plane
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coordinates"} coordinates
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electric+field"} electric field
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \;
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector"} vector
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mover
                          %mi E
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vec E
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electric+current"} electric current
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \;
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector"} vector
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mover
                          %mi J
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vec J
          %p
            With the current running in the
            = succeed "-direction" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi J
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≔
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mrow
                    %mo [
                    %mtable{:columnalign => "center", :displaystyle => "false", :rowspacing => "0.5ex"}
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi J
                            %mi x
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mn 0
                    %mo ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\vec J
                  \\;\coloneqq\;
                  \\left[
                  \\begin{array}{c}
                  J_{x}
                  \\\
                  0
                  \\end{array}
                  \\right]
          %p the statement of the classical Hall effect is that
          %ul
            %li
              not just the
              %em longitudinal field
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mi x
                    %mo ≠
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_x \neq 0
          %p but also
          %ul
            %li
              the
              %em Hall field
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi E
                        %mi y
                      %mo ≠
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_y \neq 0
          %p
            To say this more formally, recall that in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conductor"} conductor
            the current
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi J
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vec J
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+function"} linear function
            of the field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vec E
            with proportionality being the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conductivity"} conductivity
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor"} tensor
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                  %mo ×
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2 \times 2
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matrix"} matrix
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ohm%27s+law"} Ohm's law
            holds:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi J
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi σ
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mover
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\vec J \,=\, \sigma \cdot \vec E
                  \\,.
          %p Assuming that the conducting sheet has no preferred direction, the conductivity tensor is of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mrow
                    %mo [
                    %mtable{:columnalign => "center center", :displaystyle => "false", :rowspacing => "0.5ex"}
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi σ
                            %mrow
                              %mi x
                              %mi x
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi σ
                            %mrow
                              %mi x
                              %mi y
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %msub
                            %mi σ
                            %mrow
                              %mi x
                              %mi y
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi σ
                            %mi xx
                    %mo ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\sigma
                  \\;=\;
                  \\left[
                  \\begin{array}{cc}
                  \\sigma_{x x} &amp; \sigma_{x y}
                  \\\
                  \- \sigma_{x y} &amp; \sigma_{xx}
                  \\end{array}
                  \\right]
          %p
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi σ
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi σ
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mi y
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ∈
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma_{x x}, \sigma_{x y} \,\in\, \mathbb{R}
          %p
            The corresponding
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/resistivity"} resistivity
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor"} tensor
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse+matrix"} inverse matrix
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi σ
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho = \sigma^{-1}
          #eq:GenericResistivityTensor.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mrow
                    %mo [
                    %mtable{:columnalign => "center center", :displaystyle => "false", :rowspacing => "0.5ex"}
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi ρ
                            %mrow
                              %mi x
                              %mi x
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi ρ
                            %mrow
                              %mi x
                              %mi y
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %msub
                            %mi ρ
                            %mrow
                              %mi x
                              %mi y
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi ρ
                            %mrow
                              %mi y
                              %mi y
                    %mo ]
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mrow
                        %msubsup
                          %mi σ
                          %mrow
                            %mi x
                            %mi x
                          %mn 2
                        %mo +
                        %msubsup
                          %mi σ
                          %mrow
                            %mi x
                            %mi y
                          %mn 2
                  %mrow
                    %mo [
                    %mtable{:columnalign => "center center", :displaystyle => "false", :rowspacing => "0.5ex"}
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi σ
                            %mrow
                              %mi x
                              %mi x
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %msub
                            %mi σ
                            %mrow
                              %mi x
                              %mi y
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi σ
                            %mrow
                              %mi x
                              %mi y
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi σ
                            %mrow
                              %mi x
                              %mi x
                    %mo ]
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\rho
                  \\;=\;
                  \\left[
                  \\begin{array}{cc}
                  \\rho_{x x} &amp; \rho_{x y}
                  \\\
                  \- \rho_{x y} &amp; \rho_{y y}
                  \\end{array}
                  \\right]
                  \\;=\;
                  \\tfrac{1}{
                  \\sigma_{x x}^2 + \sigma_{x y}^2
                  }
                  \\left[
                  \\begin{array}{cc}
                  \\sigma_{x x} &amp; -\sigma_{x y}
                  \\\
                  \\sigma_{x y} &amp; \sigma_{x x}
                  \\end{array}
                  \\right]
                  \\,.
          %p
            in terms of which
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ohm%27s+law"} Ohm's law
            reads
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mover
                    %mi J
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\vec E \,=\, \rho \cdot \vec J
                  \\,.
          %p
            In this tensorial language, the classical Hall effect is the statement that for transverse magnetic field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo &gt;
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \gt 0
            the non-diagonal elements of the conductivity/resistivity tensors are non-vanishing, in that we have
          #eq:NonVanishingHallConductivity.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (2)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mtext Hall conductivity
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi σ
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mi y
                  %mo ≠
                  %mn 0
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mtext and
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mtext Hall resistance
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi ρ
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mi y
                  %mo ≠
                  %mn 0
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\text{Hall conductivity}
                  \\;\;
                  \\sigma_{x y} \neq 0
                  \\;\;\;\;\;\;
                  \\text{and}
                  \\;\;\;\;\;\;
                  \\text{Hall resistance}
                  \\;\;
                  \\rho_{x y} \neq 0
                  \\,.
          %p
            In this case the basic matrix relation
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:GenericResistivityTensor"} (1)
            is of some importance for understanding the measurement results in the integer quantum Hall effect
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#IdeaIntegerQuantumHallEffect"} below
            = succeed "," do
              %em longitudinal conductivity and resistivity may jointly vanish
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:LongitudinalResistivityVanishesWithLongConductivity"} (4)
            below.
          %p Concretely, the Hall resistivity turns out to be related to
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                – the number density of electrons per surface area,
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
                – the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magnetic+field"} magnetic field
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+strength"} field strength
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi e
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
                – the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electric+charge"} electric charge
                of the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electron"} electron
          %p by the formula
          #eq:HallResistivity.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (3)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ρ
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mi y
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mfrac
                    %mi B
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mi e
                  %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                    %mrow
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\rho_{x y}
                  \\;=\;
                  \\frac{
                  B
                  }{
                  n e
                  }
                  \\mathrlap{\,.}
          %h3#IdeaIntegerQuantumHallEffect Integer quantum Hall effect
          %p
            At extremely low
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/temperature"} temperature
            and extreme thin-ness of the conducting sheet, the above
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+Hall+effect"} classical Hall effect
            exhibits modifications by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanical
            effects, due to the fact that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/energy"} energy
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electrons"} electrons
            in a transverse magnetic field is
            %em quantized
            into discrete units known as
            = succeed "." do
              %em Landau levels
          %p
            Since electrons are
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermions"} fermions
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pauli+exclusion+principle"} Pauli exclusion principle
            demands that in their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ground+state"} ground state
            the electrons fill the available
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fermi+sea"} Fermi sea
            with one electron per available state, below a given energy, the “chemical potential”. (Here, due to the strong external
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magnetic+field"} magnetic field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin"} spin
            aligned along this field, so that the states in question concern just the remaining electron
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/momenta"} momenta
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+states"} quantum states
            are comprised by one Landau level.
          %p
            In the case that the electrons fill
            %em exactly
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \in \mathbb{N}
            Landau levels – one speaks of
            %em
              filling fraction
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ν
                    %mo =
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu = i
            –, the next excited state, needed for the transport of charge, is separated by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/energy+gap"} energy gap
            to the next Landau level, and hence at an integer number of exactly filled Landau levels the Hall system behaves like an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/insulator"} insulator
            with vanishing longitudinal conductivity
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi σ
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mi x
                    %mo ∼
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma_{x x} \sim 0
          %p
            What is measured in experiments is the
            = succeed "," do
              %em longitudinal resistivity
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:GenericResistivityTensor"} (1)
            with
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:NonVanishingHallConductivity"} (2)
            — also goes to zero at these points of exactly filled Landau levels:
          #eq:LongitudinalResistivityVanishesWithLongConductivity.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (4)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi R
                    %mi x
                  %mo ≔
                  %msub
                    %mi ρ
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mi x
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi σ
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                          %mi x
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi σ
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                          %mi x
                        %mn 2
                      %mo +
                      %msubsup
                        %mi σ
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                          %mi y
                        %mn 2
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %munder
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi σ
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                          %mi x
                      %mo →
                      %mn 0
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mn 0
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  R_x
                  \\coloneqq
                  \\rho_{x x}
                  \\;=\;
                  \\frac{
                  \\sigma_{x x}
                  }{
                  \\sigma^2_{x x} + \sigma^2_{x y}
                  }
                  \\;\;
                  \\underset{\sigma_{x x} \to 0}{\longrightarrow}
                  \\;\;
                  0
                  \\,.
          %p
            But the hallmark of the integer quantum Hall effect is that this vanishing of the longitudinal (conductivity and hence) resistivity is observed
            %em not just
            right when the magnetic field strength is at the critical value
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B = B_i
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/neighbourhood"} neighbourhood
            of these values:
          %center
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/IntegerQuantumHallEffect.jpg", :width => "500"}/
          %p Remarkably, the height of these Hall plateaus is an experimental constant to high precision, and is independent of the detailed nature of the underlying material, unaffected even by punching holes into the conducting sheet.
          %p
            Yet more remarkably, the explanation for the horizontal extension of these plateaux is thought to be related to
            %em impurities
            in the material — in an ideally pure conductor the quantum Hall effect is expected to be invisible! The idea is that, due to the impurities, the idealized picture of Landau levels applies only to some of the electrons in the sample, while others are “localized” at/by the imporitites; and as the magnetic field is varied it is only after the reservoir of localized electrons has changed energy levels that it becomes the turn of the “quantum Hall electrons”.
          %p To compute the Hall plateaux values:
          %p The density of available states (number per surface area) available in a Landau level is
          #eq:DensityOfLandauStates.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (5)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mfrac
                    %mi e
                    %mi h
                  %mi B
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mfrac
                    %mi B
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Φ
                        %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  d
                  \\;=\;
                  \\frac{
                  e
                  }{
                  h
                  }
                  B
                  \\;=\;
                  \\frac{B}{\Phi_0}
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                where
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi h
                      %mo =
                      %mn 2
                      %mi π
                      %mi ℏ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h = 2\pi \hbar
                is
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck%27s+constant"} Planck's constant
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Φ
                        %mn 0
                      %mo =
                      %mfrac
                        %mi h
                        %mi e
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Phi_0 = \frac{h}{e}
                is the
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    unit magnetic
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+quantum"} flux quantum
          %p hence there us room for one electron per magnetic flux quanta.
          %p
            Therefore the
            = succeed "th" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
          %ul
            %li
              there are exactly
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
              electrons per magnetic flux quanta
          %p hence when
          %ul
            %li
              the electron density
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi i
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi d
                  %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                    %mrow
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  n \,=\, i \cdot d
                  \\mathrlap{\,,}
          %p
            which, according to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:DensityOfLandauStates"} (5)
          #eq:CriticalMagneticField.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (6)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mi i
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≔
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi Φ
                    %mn 0
                  %mfrac
                    %mi n
                    %mi i
                  %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                    %mrow
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  B_i
                  \\;\coloneqq\;
                  \\Phi_0
                  \\frac{n}{i}
                  \\mathrlap{\,,}
          %p
            for which in turn, by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:HallResistivity"} (3)
          #eq:HeightOfIntegerHallPlateaux.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (7)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi ρ
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mi y
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi i
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi B
                        %mi i
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mi e
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mfrac
                    %mi h
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi e
                        %mn 2
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mi i
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (\rho_{x y})_i
                  \\;=\;
                  \\frac{
                  B_i
                  }{
                  n e
                  }
                  \\;=\;
                  \\frac{
                  h
                  }{
                  e^2
                  }
                  \\frac{1}{i}
                  \\,.
          %p
            This is hence the height of the
            = succeed "th" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            These formulas, at least, generalize immediately from (positive) integers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            to (positive)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+numbers"} rational numbers
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ν
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu
          %p
            In particular, for the 1st Landau level to be filled up to an integer fraction
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ν
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu = 1/k
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            magnetic flux quanta per electron.
          %p
            Nothing special is expected to happen at these fractional fillings of Landau level from the above understanding based all on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/energy+gap"} energy gap
            seen by non-interacting electrons (only) at the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fermi+surface"} Fermi surface
            of a filled Landau level. But electron interaction changes this picture, leading to the
            = succeed ":" do
              %em fractional quantum Hall effect
          %p
            %br/
          %h3#IdeaFQHE Fractional quantum Hall effect
          %p
            Even though the integer quantum Hall effect (
            %a{:href => "#IdeaIntegerQuantumHallEffect"}> above
            ) involves many electrons (a macroscopic number on the scale of the
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Avogadro+constant"} Avogadro constant
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fermi+sea"} Fermi sea
            surface of a filled Landau level as if they were “free” (non-interacting). That such a radical (and conceptually unjustified!) approximation works so well is surprising on a fundamental level, but is entirely common in traditional
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/solid+state+physics"} solid state physics
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Landau%27s+Fermi+liquid+theory"} Landau's Fermi liquid theory
          %p
            However, yet closer experimental analysis at yet smaller
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/temperatures"} temperatures
            shows that this approximation breaks down at some point, and that the strong interaction between the electrons makes them collectively behave in exotic ways.
          %p
            Concretely, experiments show that Hall plateaus appear not just at integer filling levels, but (smaller) Hall plateaus appear also at certain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+number"} rational
            filling fractions
          #eq:FillingFraction.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (8)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ν
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℚ
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mtext notably at
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi ν
                  %mo =
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mi q
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mtext for
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi q
                  %mo ∈
                  %mn 2
                  %mi ℕ
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\nu \in \mathbb{Q}
                  \\,,
                  \\;\;\;
                  \\text{notably at}
                  \\;\;
                  \\nu = \tfrac{1}{q}
                  \\;\text{for}\;
                  q \in 2\mathbb{N} + 1
                  \\,.
          %p
            Concretely, by the same computation as for
            = succeed "," do
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:HeightOfIntegerHallPlateaux"} (7)
          #eq:HeightOfFractionalHallPlateaux.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (9)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi ρ
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mi y
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi ν
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mfrac
                    %mi h
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi e
                        %mn 2
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mi ν
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (\rho_{x y})_\nu
                  \\;=\;
                  \\frac{
                  h
                  }{
                  e^2
                  }
                  \\frac{1}{\nu}
                  \\,.
          %p
            This experimentally observed phenomenon is thus called the
            = succeed "." do
              %em fractional quantum Hall effect
          %p
            Unfortunately, due to the general open problem of formulating and analyzing
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} non-perturbative quantum field theory
          %p What people have come up with, instead, are:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %em ad hoc
                (mental) models of how the electrons form supposedly “
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bound+states"}> bound states
                ” with magnetic flux quanta: “
                %strong> composite fermions
                ”,
              %p
                suggesting that the fractional quantum Hall effect is just the integer quantum Hall effect again, now not for plain electrons but for their exotic “fractional”
                = succeed "/quasi-hole" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-particle"} quasi-particle
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bound+states"} bound states
            %li
              %p
                some educated guesses as to the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+many-body+physics"} many-electron
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wavefunction"} wavefunction
                describing the fractional Hall
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+state"} quantum state
                –
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Laughlin+wavefunctions"} Laughlin wavefunctions
              %p which, while just guessed, are confirmed well by experiment and have in practice become the effective theory for FQH systems,
            %li
              %p
                some actual
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+field+theory"} effectice
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
                description by variants of
                = succeed "." do
                  %strong
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+Chern-Simons+theory"} abelian Chern-Simons theory
          %p
            For more on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Laughlin+wavefunctions"} Laughlin wavefunctions
            and on effective
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+Chern-Simons+theory"} abelian Chern-Simons theory
            in the FQH context, see there.
          %h3#CompositeParticlePicture Composite particle picture
          %p The simple basic assumption of the “composite particle” (CP) heuristic for explaining the FQH effect is that:
          %p
            One electron “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bound+state"}> bound to
            ” (maybe better: “grouped with”)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux"} flux
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+quantization"} quanta
            behaves like
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                a composite
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermion"} fermion
                when
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                is
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/even+number"} even
            %li
              %p
                a composite
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boson"} boson
                when
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                is
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/odd+number"} odd
          %p
            The idea behind this is, heuristically, that as such pairs of electron+flux compounds are moved around each other, the intrinsic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particle+statistics"} particle statistics
            of the pairs of electrons (according to which the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wavefunction"} wavefunction
            picks up a factor of
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Aharonov-Bohm+effect"} Aharonov Bohm phases
            of the electrons circling around the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m
            fluxes (according to which the wavefunction picks up another factor of
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn 1
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-1)^n
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mo +
                      %mi n
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (-1)^{1 + n}
                  \\,,
          %p hence for
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                a total phase
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -1
                if
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                is odd, as for fermions,
            %li
              %p
                a total phase
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} +1
                if
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                is even, as for bosons.
          %blockquote
            %p
              (Not so clear how to really justify this combination of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particle+statistics"} particle statistics
              with
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Aharonov-Bohm+effect"} Aharonov-Bohm phases
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            If one accepts this heuristics of electron+flux
            = succeed "," do
              %em composite particles
          %p
            First, at unit filling fraction
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ν
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu = 1/q
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
            odd, there are
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
            flux quanta per electron. Thinking of these as being coupled in the form of “composite bosons” (since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
            is assumed to be odd, which are indeed the primary filling fractions seen in experiment, though far from the only ones) then the effective system is now bosonic and hence may
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bose-Einstein+condensate"} condense
            to give the fractional quantum Hall phase (
            %a{:href => "#GirvinMacDonald87"}> Girvin &amp; MacDonald 1987 p 1254
            ).
          %p
            Further exposition of this perspective by
            %a{:href => "#Störmer99"} Störmer 1999
            is quoted
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Störmer99Quote"} below
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            But
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Jain89"} Jain 1989
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Jain92"} 1992
            %a{:href => "#Jain07"} 2007
            has argued that the following alternative perspective of composite
            %em fermions
            is more compelling and in any case more general and more expressive, as it neatly captures also the correct hierarchy of filling fractions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ν
                  %mo =
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu = p/q
            with
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo &gt;
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \gt 1
          %p
            First assume that the electrons each bind to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                  %mi m
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2m
            flux quanta (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m \in \mathbb{N}
            ) to make composite fermions, and
            %em then
            assume that these composite fermions
            %em themselves
            show an integer quantum Hall effect at filling number
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            such composite fermions for each
            %em remaining
            (surplus, unbound)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+quantum"} flux quantum
          %p In total then the ratio of flux quanta over electrons is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                    %mfrac
                      %mi flux
                      %mi electrons
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mn 2
                  %mi m
                  %mo ±
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\tfrac{flux}{electrons}
                  \\;=\;
                  2m \pm \tfrac{1}{p}
          %p
            (the sign choice reflecting choice of relative orientation of the magnetic field) and so the corresponding filling fractions are (
            %a{:href => "#Jain89"}> Jain 1989 p 1-2
            )
          #eq:JainSequenceOfFillingFractions.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (10)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ν
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≡
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                    %mfrac
                      %mi electrons
                      %mi flux
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi p
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                    %mfrac
                      %mi p
                      %mrow
                        %mn 2
                        %mi p
                        %mi m
                        %mo ±
                        %mn 1
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\nu
                  \\;\equiv\;
                  \\tfrac{electrons}{flux}
                  \\;=\;
                  \\frac
                  { 1 }
                  { 2 n + 1/p }
                  \\;=\;
                  \\tfrac{
                  p
                  }{
                  2 p m \pm 1
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p
            Remarkably, this
            %em Jain sequence
            formula captures a significant part of the zoo of observed filling fractions with odd denominators:
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/Jain1989-Fig1.jpg", :width => "500px"}/
              %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"}
                (from
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "#Jain89"} Jain 1989
          %p
            Further fractions have been argued to be explained this way if one assumes moreover that the composite fermions may themselves exhibit not just the integer also a fractional quantum Hall effect &amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#ChangJain04"}> Chang &amp; Jain 2004
            \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            %br/
          %p{:id => "Störmer99Quote"}
            A vivid account of the composite boson picture at odd
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+fractions"} unit fractions
            of electron-filling is given by
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#Störmer99"} Störmer 1999
          %blockquote
            %p
              “In the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fractional+quantum+Hall+effect"} FQHE
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superconductivity"} Superconductivity
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superfluidity"} superfluidity
              are of such intricate origin. To account for their occurrence one had to devise novel, sophisticated theoretical means. The emergence of the FQHE requires such a new kind of thinking. […]
            %p
              It was an important conceptual step to realize that an impinging
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magnetic+field"} magnetic field
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              could be viewed as creating tiny whirlpools, so-called
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vortices"} vortices
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+quantization"} flux quantum
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mn 0
                    %mo =
                    %mi h
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi e
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_0 = h/e
              of the magnetic field. […] Casting electron-electron correlation in terms of vortex attachment facilitates the comprehension of this intricate many-particle behavior. Regarding the vortices as little whirlpools ultimately remains a crutch for visualizing something that has no classical analog. […]
          .float_right_image{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/Stormer99-Fig14.jpg", :width => "360px"}/
          %blockquote
            %p
              At magnetic fields higher than the
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i = 1
              IQHE, the stronger magnetic field provides more
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+quanta"} flux quanta
              and hence there are more vortices than there are electrons. The Pauli principle is readily satisfied by placing one vortex onto each electron [Fig. 14(a)]—but there are more vortices available. The electron system can considerably reduce its electrostatic Coulomb energy by placing more vortices onto each electron [Fig. 14(b)]. More vortices on an electron generate a bigger surrounding whirlpool, pushing further away all fellow electrons, thereby reducing the repulsive energy. […]
            %p
              Vortices are the expression of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+quanta"} flux quanta
              in the 2D electron system, and each vortex can be thought of as having been created by a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+quantum"} flux quantum
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+quanta"} flux quanta
              themselves. Then the placement of vortices onto electrons becomes equivalent to the attachment of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magnetic+flux+quanta"} magnetic flux quanta
              to the carriers. Electrons plus
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+quanta"} flux quanta
              can be viewed as new entities, which have come to be called composite particles, CPs.
            %p
              As these objects move through the liquid, the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+quanta"} flux quanta
              act as an invisible shield against other electrons. Replacing the system of highly interacting electrons by a system of electrons with such a “guard ring” – compliments of the magnetic field – removes most of the electron-electron interaction from the problem and leads to composite particles which are almost void of mutual interactions. It is a minor miracle that such a transformation from a very complex many-particle problem of well-known objects (electrons in a magnetic field) to a much simpler single-particle problem of rather complex objects (electrons plus
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+quanta"} flux quanta
            %p
              CPs act differently from bare electrons. All of the external magnetic field has been incorporated into the particles via
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+quantum"} flux quantum
              attachment to the electrons. Therefore, from the perspective of CPs, the magnetic field has disappeared and they no longer are subject to it. They inhabit an apparently field-free 2D plane. Yet more importantly, the attached
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+quanta"} flux quanta
              change the character of the particles from fermions to bosons and back to fermions. […]
          .float_right_image{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/Stormer99-Fig16.jpg", :width => "360px"}/
          %blockquote
            %p#StormerOnThirdFractionModel
              As the magnetic field deviates from exactly
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ν
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu = 1/3
              filling to higher fields, more vortices are being created (Fig. 16). They are not attached to any electrons, since this would disturb the symmetry of the condensed state. The amount of charge deficit in any of these vortices amounts to exactly 1/3 of an electronic charge. These quasiholes (whirlpool in the electron lake) are effectively positive charges as compared to the negatively charged electrons. An analogous argument can be made for magnetic fields slightly below
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ν
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu = 1/3
              and the creation of quasielectrons of negative charge
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi e
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mn 3
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e/3
            %p Plateau formation in the FQHE arises, in analogy to plateau formation in the IQHE from potential fluctuations and the resulting localization of carriers. In the case of the FQHE the carriers are not electrons, but, instead, the bizarre fractionally charged quasiparticles.
            %p
              \&amp;lbrack;end of excerpt from
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "#Störmer99"} Störmer 1999
          %p
            %br/
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#BraidingPhase Braiding phase
          %p
            In an FQH system, when one quasi-hole (unpaired
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+quantum"} flux quantum
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+adiabatic+theorem"} adiabatically
            moved past another, hence one quasi-hole moved along
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hemisphere"} half
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle"} circle
            centered at the other, then their joint
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+state"} quantum state
            picks up a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Berry+phase"} Berry phase
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi e
                    %mrow
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} i
                      %mi π
                      %mi θ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  e^{ \mathrm{i} \pi \theta}
          %p
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/U%281%29"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(1)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \subset \mathbb{C}
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+anyons"} abelian anyons
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 30px 10px"}
            %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/FQH-BraidingPhase-250530.jpg", :width => "580px"}/
              %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"}
                (graphics from
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "#SS25AlgTop"} SS25
          %p
            At
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+fraction"} unit fraction
            filling factors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ν
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu = 1/q
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/angle"} angle
            of that
            %em braiding phase
            is the same fraction of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pi"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
            (up to a sign reflecting the orientation of the braiding and the choice of quasi-holes over quasi-particles)
          #eq:BraidingAngleAtUnitFractionFillingFactor.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (11)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ν
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi q
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi q
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi θ
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ±
                  %mi π
                  %mi ν
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\nu = 1/q,
                  \\;\;
                  q \in \mathbb{Z}
                  \\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;
                  \\Rightarrow
                  \\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;
                  \\theta
                  \\;=\;
                  \\pm
                  \\pi \nu
                  \\,,
          %p
            this according to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Berry+phase"} Berry phase
            computation via the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+adiabatic+theorem"} quantum adiabatic theorem
            %a{:href => "#ArovasSchriefferWilczek84"} Arovas, Schrieffer &amp; Wilczek 1984 (12)
            (also claimed in
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Halperin84"} Halperin 1984 p 1584
          %p
            It appears that
            %a{:href => "#Su86"} Su 1986 (3)
            claims that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi θ
                  %mo =
                  %mo ±
                  %mi π
                  %mi ν
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta = \pm \pi \nu
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:BraidingAngleAtUnitFractionFillingFactor"} (11)
            should hold for general filling fractions
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ν
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu
          %p
            Motivated by
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Halperin84"} Halperin 1984
            %a{:href => "#GoldhaberJain95"} Goldhaber &amp; Jain 1995
            claim, reviewed by
            %a{:href => "#Jain07"} Jain 2007 (9.46)
            that for filling factors in the Jain sequence
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:JainSequenceOfFillingFractions"} (10)
            the braiding angle is
          #eq:BraidingAngleAtJainSequenceFillingFactor.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (12)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ν
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mfrac
                    %mi n
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi p
                      %mi n
                      %mo ±
                      %mn 1
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi p
                  %mo ,
                  %mi n
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi θ
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ±
                  %mi π
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi p
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi p
                      %mi n
                      %mo ±
                      %mn 1
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\nu \,=\,
                  \\frac{
                  n
                  }{
                  2 p n \pm 1
                  }
                  \\;\;
                  p, n \in \mathbb{Z}
                  \\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;
                  \\Rightarrow
                  \\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;
                  \\theta
                  \\;=\;
                  \\pm
                  \\pi
                  \\frac{
                  2 p
                  }{
                  2 p n \pm 1
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              While in the special case
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 1
              the formula
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:BraidingAngleAtJainSequenceFillingFactor"} (12)
              still differs from
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:BraidingAngleAtUnitFractionFillingFactor"} (11)
              it does so only by a unit shift
            #eq:ShiftInBraidingAngles.maruku-equation
              %span.maruku-eq-number (13)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∓
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mn 2
                        %mi p
                      %mrow
                        %mn 2
                        %mi p
                        %mo ±
                        %mn 1
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo =
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mrow
                        %mn 2
                        %mi p
                        %mo ±
                        %mn 1
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ∓
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mn 1
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mp
                    \\frac{
                    2 p
                    }{
                    2 p \pm 1
                    }
                    \\;=\;
                    \\frac{
                    1
                    }{
                    2 p \pm 1
                    }
                    \\,\mp\,
                    1
                    \\,,
            %p
              and hence by a sign of the corresponding braiding phase, which
              %a{:href => "#Jain07"} Jain 2007 (9.49)
              attributes but to a different convention of normalizing the corresponding
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Laughlin+wavefunction"} Laughlin wavefunction
            %p
          %p
            For the two cases
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ν
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu = 1/3
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ν
                    %mo =
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mn 5
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu = 2/5
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:BraidingAngleAtJainSequenceFillingFactor"} (12)
            appears to be confirmed first by numerical computations (referenced around
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Jain07"} Jain 2007 (9.46)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiments"} experiments
            for the cases
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ν
                  %mo ∈
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} {
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mn 3
                  %mo ,
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 2
                      %mn 5
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu \in \big\{ \tfrac{1}{3}, \tfrac{2}{5} \big\}
            (see
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#NakamuraEtAl20"} Nakamura et al. 2020
            %a{:href => "#NakamuraEtAl23"} Nakamura et al. 2023 p 1,7
            and further references
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#ObservationOfAnyonsInFQH"} below
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              More precisely, these interferometry experiments measure the effect of full rotations and hence
              %em twice
              the braiding angle
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modulo"} modulo
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi π
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2\pi
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:ShiftInBraidingAngles"} (13)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ν
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu = 1/3
              do not distinguish between braiding angles
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ±
                    %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                      %mfrac
                        %mn 1
                        %mn 3
                    %mi π
                    %mo =
                    %mi ν
                    %mi π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pm \tfrac{1}{3}\pi  = \nu \pi
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∓
                      %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                        %mfrac
                          %mn 2
                          %mn 3
                      %mi π
                      %mo =
                      %mn 2
                      %mi ν
                      %mi π
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mp \tfrac{2}{3} \pi = 2\nu \pi
            %p
              Moreover, for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ν
                    %mo =
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mn 5
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu = 2/5
              the formula
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:BraidingAngleAtJainSequenceFillingFactor"} (12)
              happens to coincide with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi θ
                    %mo =
                    %mi ν
                    %mi π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta = \nu \pi
              on the nose.
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              The
              %a{:href => "abelian+Chern-Simons+theory#HierarchicalKMatrixFormalism"} hierarchical K-matrix formalism
              seems to allow more general relations between filling factor and braiding phase, cf.
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "#Wen95"} Wen 1995 (2.30-1)
          %p
            %br/
