          %h4#ReferencesMicroscopicModelsForFQHE Phenomenological models
          %p Phenomenological models for the fractional quantum Hall effect:
          %p
            The original
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Laughlin+wavefunction"} Laughlin wavefunction
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+B.+Laughlin"}> Robert B. Laughlin
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Anomalous Quantum Hall Effect: An Incompressible Quantum Fluid with Fractionally Charged Excitations
              %strong 50
              (1983) 1395 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevLett.50.1395"}> doi:10.1103/PhysRevLett.50.1395
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p The Halperin multi-component model:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bertrand+I.+Halperin"}> Bertrand I. Halperin
              \:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Theory of the Quantized Hall Conductance
              %strong 56
              (1983) 75-102 [
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.5169/seals-115362"}> doi:10.5169/seals-115362
              ,
              = succeed "]" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Halperin-TheoryOfQHE.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %p
            The Haldane-Halperin hierachy model for FQH system at non-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+fraction"} unit
            filling fractions:
          %ul
            %li#Haldane83
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F.+Duncan+M.+Haldane"}> F. Duncan M. Haldane
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Fractional Quantization of the Hall Effect: A Hierarchy of Incompressible Quantum Fluid States
                %strong 51
                (1983) 605 [
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevLett.51.605"}> doi:10.1103/PhysRevLett.51.605
                ]
            %li#Halperin84
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bertrand+Halperin"}> Bertrand Halperin
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Statistics of Quasiparticles and the Hierarchy of Fractional Quantized Hall States
                %strong 52
                (1984) 1583 [
                %a{:href => "https://journals.aps.org/prl/abstract/10.1103/PhysRevLett.52.1583"}> doi:10.1103/PhysRevLett.52.1583
                ]
          %p
            The composite-fermion model (CF) which explains the FQHE as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integer+quantum+Hall+effect"} integer quantum Hall effect
            not of the bare electrons but of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-particles"} quasi-particles
            which they form (for reasons not explained by the model):
          %ul
            %li#Jain89
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jainendra+K.+Jain"}> Jainendra K. Jain
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Composite-fermion approach for the fractional quantum Hall effect
                %strong 63
                (1989) 199 [
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevLett.63.199"}> doi:10.1103/PhysRevLett.63.199
                ]
            %li#Jain92
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jainendra+K.+Jain"}> Jainendra K. Jain
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Microscopic theory of the fractional quantum Hall effect
                %strong 41
                (1992) 105-146 [
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1080/00018739200101483"}> doi:10.1080/00018739200101483
                ]
            %li#Jain07
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jainendra+K.+Jain"}> Jainendra K. Jain
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Composite Fermions
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9780511607561"} doi:10.1017/CBO9780511607561
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Jain07-ch5.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Jain07-ch9.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
                = succeed "]" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Jain07-ch12.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %p
            On FQH
            %em of
            composite fermions:
          %ul
            %li#ChangJain04
              C.-C Chang,
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jainendra+K.+Jain"} Jainendra K. Jain
              = succeed "," do
                %em Microscopic origin of the next generation fractional quantum Hall effect
              %strong 92
              (2004) 196806 [
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevLett.92.196806"}> doi:10.1103/PhysRevLett.92.196806
              ,
              = succeed "]" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/cond-mat/0404079"} arXiv:cond-mat/0404079
          %p Review:
          %ul
            %li#Jain2020
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jainendra+K.+Jain"}> Jainendra K. Jain
              \:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Thirty Years of Composite Fermions and Beyond
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "#HalperinJain20"} Halperin &amp; Jain 2020
              = succeed "," do
                %em Fractional Quantum Hall Effects – New Developments
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2011.13488"} arXiv:2011.13488
              = succeed "]" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1142/11751"} doi:10.1142/11751
          %p Further discussion:
          %ul
            %li#Jain14
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jainendra+K.+Jain"}> Jainendra K. Jain
              \:
              = succeed "," do
                %em A note contrasting two microscopic theories of the fractional quantum Hall effect
              %strong 88
              (2014) 915-929 [
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/s12648-014-0491-9"}> doi:10.1007/s12648-014-0491-9
              ,
              = succeed "]" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1403.5415"} arXiv:1403.5415
          %p Discussion highlighting the lack of microscopic explanation of these phenomenological models:
          %ul
            %li#Jacak21
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Janusz+E.+Jacak"}> Janusz E. Jacak
              \:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Topological approach to electron correlations at fractional quantum Hall effect
              %strong 430
              (2021) 168493 [
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.aop.2021.168493"}> doi:10.1016/j.aop.2021.168493
              ]
          %blockquote
            %p
              \&amp;lbrack;p 3:&amp;rbrack; “Though the Laughlin function very well approximates the true ground state at
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ν
                      %mo =
                      %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                        %mfrac
                          %mn 1
                          %mi q
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu = \tfrac{1}{q}
            %p “The so-called HH (Halperin–Haldane) model of consecutive generations of Laughlin states of anyonic quasiparticle excitations from the preceding Laughlin state has been abandoned early because of the rapid growth of the daughter quasiparticle size, which quickly exceeded the sample size.”
            %p
              “the Halperin multicomponent theory and of the CF model advanced the understanding of correlations in FQHE, however, on a phenomenological level only. CFs were assumed to be hypothetical quasi-particles consisting of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electrons"} electrons
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+quanta"} flux quanta
              of an auxiliary fictitious magnetic field pinned to them. The origin of this field and the manner of attachment of its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+quanta"} flux quanta
              to electrons have been neither explained nor discussed.”
          %p
            Introducing
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+Chern-Simons+theory"} abelian Chern-Simons theory
            to the picture:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Ana Lopez,
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eduardo+Fradkin"} Eduardo Fradkin
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Fractional quantum Hall effect and Chern-Simons gauge theories
                %strong 44
                (1991) 5246 [
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevB.44.5246"}> doi:10.1103/PhysRevB.44.5246
                ]
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Xiao-Gang+Wen"}> Xiao-Gang Wen
                ,
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anthony+Zee"} Anthony Zee
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Classification of Abelian quantum Hall states and matrix formulation of topological fluids
                %strong 46
                (1992) 2290 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevB.46.2290"}> doi:10.1103/PhysRevB.46.2290
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %div
            %h3#ReferencesAbCSForFQHE Abelian Chern-Simons for fractional quantum Hall effect
            %p
              The idea of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+Chern-Simons+theory"} abelian Chern-Simons theory
              as an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+field+theory"} effective field theory
              exhibiting the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fractional+quantum+Hall+effect"} fractional quantum Hall effect
              (abelian
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+order"} topological order
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steven+M.+Girvin"}> Steven M. Girvin
                  , around (10.7.15) in:
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em Summary, Omissions and Unanswered Questions
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em The Quantum Hall Effect
                  = succeed "]" do
                    %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4612-3350-3"} doi:10.1007/978-1-4612-3350-3
              %li#GirvinMacDonald87
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steven+M.+Girvin"}> Steven M. Girvin
                  ,
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Allan+H.+MacDonald"} Allan H. MacDonald
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em Off-diagonal long-range order, oblique confinement, and the fractional quantum Hall effect
                  %strong 58
                  12 (1987) (1987) 1252-1255 [
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevLett.58.1252"}> doi:10.1103/PhysRevLett.58.1252
                  ]
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Shou+Cheng+Zhang"}> Shou Cheng Zhang
                  , T. H. Hansson S. Kivelson:
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em Effective-Field-Theory Model for the Fractional Quantum Hall Effect
